def spend_coins(current_coins):
    coins = current_coins - 2
    return coins

coins = 10
coins = spend_coins(coins)
print(coins)


You:


Used a parameter: current_coins
Did the math inside the function: current_coins - 2
Returned the result and stored it back into the global coins
Printed 8, which matches 10 - 2

This is a clean example of:


local variable (current_coins, and even coins inside the function)
global variable (coins outside)
passing data in and getting a result back
